  <!-- Section 5: Production Timeline -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Production Timeline for Ecommerce Brands | Plan Your Launch</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Ecommerce brands live and die by launch dates. Miss your holiday launch window and you lose the entire season. Launch too early without inventory ready and you damage customer trust with backorders. Most manufacturers give vague timelines like "8-12 weeks" that leave you guessing. Let's break down the actual timeline with specific durations so you can plan backward from your launch date with confidence.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Tech pack finalization takes 1-2 weeks. This is where you lock in measurements, fabric specifications, and construction details. Rush this step and you'll pay for it in sampling rounds. Clear, complete tech packs reduce sampling time by 50%. If you don't have a tech pack, our <a href="/tech-pack-service">tech pack service</a> can create professional specifications within this timeframe. Approve your tech pack before moving forward - changes later cause delays.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Fabric sourcing requires 1-2 weeks. For standard fabrics like cotton jersey or polyester blends, this is straightforward. For custom fabrics with specific weaves or sustainable materials, allow 2-3 weeks. You'll receive lab dips (fabric swatches) for color approval. Approve lab dips promptly - delays here push back your entire production. Keep fabric on file for reorders to skip this step on future runs.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Pre-production sampling takes 2-3 weeks. This is your first physical sample - the moment your design becomes real. Review it carefully for fit, fabric quality, and construction. Mark up the sample with specific feedback. "Make the sleeve longer" is less helpful than "Increase sleeve length by 2 inches at shoulder seam." Specific feedback reduces revision rounds. Expect 1-2 revision rounds before final approval.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Revisions if needed add 1-2 weeks. This is where most timelines blow up. Vague feedback causes multiple revision rounds. Be specific in your first revision request. If the sample needs major changes, consider whether your tech pack was clear. Good tech packs minimize revision needs. Once you approve the sample, no more changes - modifications after approval restart the timeline.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Bulk production lasts 3-4 weeks. This is actual manufacturing of your order. The factory cuts, sews, and finishes your garments. During this period, request photo updates every week. Seeing production progress catches problems early. If issues arise, address them immediately - waiting until the end causes delays. Most problems are fixable if caught mid-production.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Quality inspection takes 3-5 days. Before shipment, conduct an AQL 2.5 inspection. This statistically valid sampling method checks a representative portion of your order against quality standards. Review the QC report for measurements, color consistency, stitching, and overall finish. If quality fails, don't accept the shipment - it's cheaper to fix it now than deal with returns later.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Sea freight to the USA requires 4-5 weeks. This is often the longest single component of the timeline. Air freight takes 3-5 days but costs 5-10x more. Use air freight only for urgent reorders or high-value items. For standard production, sea freight keeps costs manageable. Track your container through the shipping line's website - know when it arrives so you can arrange customs clearance promptly.
      </p>
      
