  <!-- Section 6: Product Strategy -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Ecommerce Clothing Brand Product Strategy | What Sells Online</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Not all clothing products perform equally in ecommerce. Some categories have higher return rates, lower margins, or limited Instagram appeal. Understanding what works online helps you choose products that maximize your chances of success. Competitors completely ignore this topic - they talk about "how to find manufacturers" but never "what should I actually manufacture." This section fills that gap.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        High-margin basics like hoodies, t-shirts, and sweatshirts are ideal for ecommerce. They're easy to photograph clearly, size runs are straightforward, and return rates are lower than complex garments. A hoodie is a hoodie - customers know what they're getting. This predictability reduces returns and negative reviews. These basics also have broad appeal across demographics, giving you a larger potential market.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Niche activewear including leggings, sports bras, and sets performs exceptionally well online. This category has loyal communities, high repeat purchase rates, and is Instagram-native. Activewear customers follow brands on social media, engage with content, and become repeat buyers. The technical nature of activewear also justifies premium pricing - customers expect to pay more for performance fabrics. This combination of community and margins makes activewear a top ecommerce choice.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Streetwear staples like cargo pants, bomber jackets, and coord sets work well for trend-driven brands. These products are inherently Instagram-friendly, lend themselves to limited drops, and the scarcity model works in ecommerce. Announce a drop of 200 cargo pants, sell out in 48 hours, and build hype for the next drop. This model creates urgency and FOMO (fear of missing out) that drives sales. Streetwear also allows for higher pricing due to trend value.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Sustainable basics including organic tees, bamboo loungewear, and recycled fabrics appeal to the growing eco-conscious segment. These customers are willing to pay premium prices for sustainability, giving you higher margins. The sustainability story also provides marketing content - customers share their values through their purchases. This segment has lower competition than mainstream categories and higher customer loyalty once acquired.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Pet clothing is a fast-growing, low-competition niche with strong ecommerce potential. Pet owners treat their animals like family and spend freely on them. Pet clothing is an impulse purchase, a gifting category, and highly shareable on social media. Who doesn't stop scrolling to see a dog in a cute outfit? The category is underserved by major retailers, leaving room for niche brands to capture market share.
      </p>
      
