  <!-- Section 8: Quality Control -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Quality Control for Ecommerce | Why It Matters More Than Wholesale</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Quality control is where ecommerce and wholesale brands diverge most dramatically. In wholesale, a buyer receives a shipment, finds quality issues, and quietly works with the manufacturer on resolution. The public never knows. In ecommerce, every customer is a potential reviewer. One bad batch generates dozens of 1-star reviews on your Shopify store that permanently damage your brand. This is why QC matters more for ecommerce - your reputation is public and permanent.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        AQL (Acceptable Quality Limit) is the international standard for quality inspection. An AQL 2.5 level means the inspection accepts shipments with no more than 2.5% defects. This sounds technical, but practically it means: for every 100 garments inspected, no more than 2-3 should have defects. This standard balances quality with realistic manufacturing capabilities. Lower AQL levels (1.5 or 1.0) are possible but increase cost - AQL 2.5 is the sweet spot for ecommerce.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        What to check during inspection: measurements against your size chart, color consistency across the batch, stitching quality (no loose threads, consistent tension), fabric quality (no flaws or pilling), and overall construction. Document everything with photos. If measurements are off by 0.5 inches, note it. If color varies between pieces, photograph the difference. Detailed documentation helps the factory fix issues and provides evidence if disputes arise.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Pre-shipment photo reports are your quality insurance. Before the factory ships, request photos of randomly selected pieces from your order. Review these photos for the same quality criteria. If you spot issues, request additional photos or a video call to see more pieces. Catching problems before shipping saves weeks of return logistics. Our <a href="/quality-inspection-service">quality inspection service</a> provides comprehensive photo reports with every shipment.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        What to do if quality fails: don't accept the shipment. Document the defects thoroughly with photos and measurements. Request the factory to rework or replace the defective pieces at their expense. This is why payment terms matter - if you've paid 100% upfront, you have no leverage. With standard 30/70 terms, you withhold the balance until quality issues are resolved. Be firm but reasonable - manufacturers make mistakes, but they should fix them.
      </p>
      <p class="section__text">
        Why ordering from the same factory matters for consistency: different factories use different fabric suppliers, sewing techniques, and quality standards. Your batch 2 from Factory B will look different from batch 1 from Factory A. This inconsistency confuses customers and generates "quality dropped" reviews. Stick with one factory for each product line. They maintain your approved samples, use consistent materials, and understand your quality expectations. Use our <a href="/tools/aql-calculator">AQL calculator</a> to determine inspection sample sizes for your orders.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

